{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And a man who takes his sister, etc. — it is ḥesed.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): the act of lying with is denominated by the term 'taking,' for the man who takes a woman with the intention of lying with her — it is said of him that he 'takes' her. So too above it says ״ואיש אשר יקח את אשה ואת אמה״, and likewise ״איש אשר יקח את אשת אחיו״ — all of them are euphemisms for lying with. And it says ״חסד הוא״ in the sense of (Proverbs 25:10): ״יחסדך שומע״, and the Targum renders ״חרפה״ (Genesis 34) as ״חסודא״ — that is to say, an ugly thing." "<b>And the Ramban</b>, of blessed memory, explained that <b>חסד</b> should not be removed from its plain sense, for it denotes the intensification of a matter, whether for praise or for blame. For one who does a very good and worthy deed — it is called חסד by way of intensification, to magnify the good; and likewise one who does a very evil and disgraceful deed — it is also called חסד to magnify the evil. And this is what it says, ״חסד הוא״ — for he corrupted and made foul the deed and intensified the ugliness." "<b>And by way of the Midrash:</b> <b>And a man who takes his sister, etc. — it is ḥesed</b> — this is Cain, who married his sister. ״חסד הוא״ — it is ḥesed in the literal sense, as it is said (Psalms 89): ״כי אמרתי עולם חסד יבנה״. From here they said: the daughter was fitting for the first Adam, but he did an act of ḥesed with Cain and gave him his daughter. And this is what it says, ״חסד הוא״ — for were it not so, the world would not have been settled."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:27:27.864216303Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}